  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            Spanish intonation involves three basic pitch levels: high, middle and low. \nStatements often begin at a high pitch and gradually fall, while questions may rise at the end. \nThe middle pitch level indicates continuation or parenthetical information, and the low pitch often signals the end of a statement. \nBecause English has different intonation patterns, English speakers must practise Spanish intonation using words and sentences, paying attention to rising and falling patterns. \nPractising with nonsense words and syllables can help you focus on pitch without worrying about vocabulary.\n"
    }
